      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Trajan</strong><br>\nTrajan was born on September 18, 53 AD, in the Roman province of Hispania, making him the first Roman emperor of provincial origin. His father was a successful general and the first in his family to enter the Senate, which paved the way for Trajan's future successes. Raised in a military environment, Trajan followed in his father's footsteps and began his career as a tribune under the emperor Domitian.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rise to Power</strong><br>\nAfter the death of Emperor Domitian, Trajan's career continued to flourish under Emperor Nerva, who adopted Trajan as his son and successor. This was a politically savvy move, as Trajan was popular with the army and the Roman people. His adoption in 97 AD assured a smooth transition of power, and Trajan became emperor when Nerva died in 98 AD.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Military Campaigns</strong><br>\nTrajan is well-known for his military conquests, which expanded the Roman Empire to its greatest territorial extent. He waged two major wars against the Dacians, who were led by their king Decebalus. The First Dacian War ended in 102 AD with Roman victory, and the Second Dacian War concluded in 106 AD, after which Dacia became a Roman province.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
