      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Public Works and Administration</strong><br>\nAs emperor, Trajan implemented extensive public building programs and social welfare policies. He constructed new roads, aqueducts, and buildings, including Trajan's Forum, Trajan's Market, and Trajan's Column, which commemorates his victory in Dacia. His rule is also marked by efforts to provide state assistance to poor parents for raising and educating their children.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Relationship with the Senate</strong><br>\nTrajan maintained a good relationship with the Roman Senate. He often consulted the Senate on matters of state and respected traditional senatorial privileges, which helped to stabilize his rule and made him popular among Rome's ruling class. His respectful approach to governance is why the Senate later declared him optimus princeps, meaning \"the best ruler.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>Economic Policies</strong><br>\nTrajan's economic policies were focused on the welfare of the empire. His conquests increased Rome's wealth through the acquisition of Dacia's gold mines. He also devalued the Roman currency slightly to increase state revenues, which he used to fund his building projects and welfare programs, ensuring broad support from the populace.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
