            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Cultural Impact</strong><br>\nDuring Trajan's reign, the Roman Empire experienced a flourishing of culture and literature. The emperor encouraged the arts and learning, which led to the creation of enduring works by writers like Pliny the Younger and Tacitus. Trajan's support for the arts demonstrated his belief in the importance of cultural prosperity for the empire's legacy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Trajan's Succession</strong><br>\nWithout any biological children to succeed him, Trajan chose to adopt Hadrian as his heir. This choice continued the trend of adoptive succession, which ensured that capable and experienced individuals would lead Rome. Hadrian was with Trajan during his final campaign in the East and took the helm of the empire after Trajan's death in 117 AD.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nTrajan died on August 8, 117 AD, after suffering a stroke in the city of Selinus. He left behind an empire at its peak in terms of economic prosperity, territorial expansion, and cultural achievements. His successors faced the challenge of maintaining the high standards and vast territories that he had established, a testament to his effective leadership and vision for Rome.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
